## Goal
Make the "could not remember" notification show both the punycode and Unicode forms of an IDN host, and close test gaps around punycode decoding, nested JWT claims and the rules-file lock (DEV-50).

## Problems
None

## Decision
`notifyRememberFailed` in `cmd/portero/app.go` now formats the host with `rules.HostForms`. This reuses the existing display logic, so a lookalike host such as `xn--80ak6aa92e.com` shows next to its Unicode form, `аррӏе.com`, instead of only the punycode.

Tests added:
- A notification test asserting both forms appear in the body.
- A `HostForms` case for `xn--abc-`, where nothing follows the last hyphen and the label decodes to plain ASCII.
- A fuzz test, `FuzzPunycodeRoundTrip`, checking that decoding an encoded string returns the original.
- An unwrap case where a repeated claim sits only at nested depth, so the top-level `target_uri` claim wins.
- `internal/rulesfile/lock_unix_test.go`, unix-only. It holds `flock` through a separate file descriptor, so the lock behaves like one held by another process. It checks that `Lock` times out with "held by another writer" within a bounded wait, and that `Lock` succeeds once the holder releases.

The lock test shortens `lockWait` and restores it afterwards, so it stays fast.

